Share the source text of nested functions.

Every function keeps its source text for Function.prototype.toString.
QuickJS copies each function's own text (js_strndup when its parse ends),
so the text of a function nested n levels deep is held n times. Scripts
written as one outer function around everything (module wrappers, IIFE
bundles) held their text twice or more: on a Wikipedia article the
function records took 3.9 MB, of which bytecode was 0.4 MB.

The text now lives in a reference-counted JSSourceText made for the
outermost function that has source. js_create_function runs
js_fd_share_source for a function before it creates the child functions;
a child whose text lies inside the nearest enclosing function's text
(offsets in the parser's buffer, then compared byte for byte) takes a
reference and an offset instead of its own copy. JSFunctionBytecode.source
points into the shared text; toString and the bytecode writer read it as
before. A function read from bytecode gets a text of its own.

Measured: the same article's script heap fell from 10.6 MB to 8.7 MB.

